          "type": "turn",
          "question": "my poo has been really dark, almost black and sticky, for three days and I feel light headed when i stand",
          "answer": "**Please get medical care today. If you faint, feel confused, have chest pain or a racing heartbeat, or pass more black stool, call emergency services (999 in the UK, 911 in the US) now.**\n\nThe triage tool rated this \"routine,\" but I don't think that rating fits what you describe. Black, sticky stool that has lasted three days, together with feeling light-headed when you stand, is the combination the guidance flags for urgent assessment. The gastrointestinal bleeding guidance says that blood from the stomach or upper gut \"arrives black and tarry rather than red\" and that this kind of bleeding \"frequently accompanies a larger volume of loss than the appearance suggests.\" It also says the safer assumption is bleeding until assessment says otherwise. The GERD guideline lists black tarry stools as a feature that calls for prompt endoscopy (a camera test of the upper gut) rather than waiting.\n\nThe same guidance notes that iron supplements and bismuth medicines can darken stool. Only a clinician can tell which applies to you, so don't assume that is the cause.\n\nWhile you wait for care:\n- Sit or lie down, and get up slowly if you must stand.\n- Don't drive yourself.\n- Don't take new medicines, including painkillers, unless a clinician or pharmacist has told you to.\n\nThe documents I searched did not cover what to do about the light-headedness itself, your likely causes, or what treatment would involve. Those questions are for the clinician who assesses you.\n\n*Sources: Gastrointestinal bleeding (gastroenterology and emergency medicine society guidance); GERD: empiric initial management and alarm features (gastroenterology society guideline).*\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
